/-!
# Simplicity of the canonical permutation model of M₁₂

The group generated by the two canonical twelve-point permutations is simple. The proof
uses explicit subgroups of orders 64, 27, 5 and 11, the full prime parts of its independently
proved order 95040. Triangular power-conjugate relations and injective bounded normal forms
certify their orders. Every prime-order element of these subgroups is conjugate in the
canonical model to one of seven representatives. For each representative, a checked
straight-line program recovers both canonical generators from its conjugates.

All permutation and word equalities are checked by the kernel. The data were generated
independently from the two permutations already defining the model; the search is not
part of the proof. This does not assert finiteness or simplicity of the abstract presentation.

The proof consumes the Sylow-local normal-generation criterion and bounded power-conjugate
normal-form theory. See Holt, Eick and O'Brien, Handbook of Computational Group Theory,
Chapters 5 and 8, for these standard computational group-theoretic methods.
-/

public section

